Zhang Shizhi, the Commandant of Justice, was a native of Duyang, with the courtesy name Ji. He lived with his elder brother Zhong. He became a mounted attendant through his wealth and served Emperor Wen for ten years without promotion, and was not well known. Shizhi said, "Having been an official for so long has reduced Zhong's property, and I have not achieved anything." He wanted to resign and return home. Zhonglang General Yuan Ang knew he was worthy and regretted his departure, so he requested that Shizhi be transferred to fill the position of Usher. After the court session, Shizhi took the opportunity to speak on matters of expediency. Emperor Wen said, "Keep it low, do not make lofty arguments, make it feasible for the present." Then Shizhi spoke at length about the affairs between the Qin and Han dynasties, why Qin lost and why Han prospered. Emperor Wen praised him and appointed Shizhi as Chief Usher.

Shizhi accompanied the emperor on a tour and climbed to the tiger enclosure. The emperor asked the Commandant of Shanglin about the records of the various animals, but after more than ten questions, the commandant looked around and could not answer at all. The tiger enclosure keeper, standing nearby, answered on behalf of the commandant in great detail, wanting to show his ability to respond fluently without end. Emperor Wen said, "Shouldn't officials be like this? The commandant is worthless!" He then ordered Shizhi to appoint the keeper as the Director of Shanglin. After a long pause, Shizhi stepped forward and said, "Your Majesty, what kind of man do you consider the Marquis of Jiang, Zhou Bo?" The emperor said, "An elder of integrity." He then asked again, "What about the Marquis of Dongyang, Zhang Xiangru?" The emperor again said, "An elder of integrity." Shizhi said, "The Marquis of Jiang and the Marquis of Dongyang are called elders of integrity, yet when these two speak of affairs, they cannot get the words out. How could they imitate this keeper with his glib tongue and quick speech! Moreover, Qin relied on clerical officials, and officials vied to outdo each other in haste and harsh scrutiny, but the result was mere formalities without genuine compassion. Because of this, the faults were not heard, and decline continued until the Second Emperor, when the empire collapsed like a mound of earth. Now if Your Majesty promotes this keeper because of his eloquence, I fear the world will follow the trend, vying for eloquence without substance. Moreover, the people are influenced by their superiors faster than shadows and echoes, so actions and decisions must not be careless." Emperor Wen said, "Well said." He then stopped and did not appoint the keeper.

When the emperor boarded his carriage, he summoned Shizhi to ride as a companion, and slowly asked him about the faults of Qin. Shizhi answered frankly and truthfully. When they reached the palace, the emperor appointed Shizhi as Commandant of the Palace Gate.

Soon after, the Crown Prince and the King of Liang rode together into the court and did not dismount at the Sima Gate. Shizhi then pursued and stopped them, preventing them from entering the palace gate. He then impeached them for not dismounting at the public gate, showing disrespect, and submitted the memorial. When Empress Dowager Bo heard of this, Emperor Wen removed his cap and apologized, saying, "I have not taught my sons carefully." Empress Dowager Bo then sent an envoy with an edict to pardon the Crown Prince and the King of Liang, and only then were they allowed to enter. Because of this, Emperor Wen regarded Shizhi as extraordinary and appointed him as Chief of the Palace Attendants.

Soon after, he became a General of the Palace. He accompanied the emperor to Baling, where they stood at the northern edge overlooking the latrine. At that time, Lady Shen accompanied them. The emperor pointed out the road to Xinfeng to Lady Shen and said, "This is the road to Handan." He had Lady Shen play the zither, and the emperor himself leaned on the zither and sang, feeling deeply sorrowful and nostalgic. He turned to the assembled ministers and said, "Alas! If I use the stone of the northern mountains for my outer coffin, and use ramie and silk wadding to fill the gaps, and lacquer it all, how could it be moved?" All those around said, "Excellent." Shizhi stepped forward and said, "If there is something desirable inside, even if sealed with the southern mountains, there will still be a gap; if there is nothing desirable inside, even without a stone coffin, what is there to worry about!" Emperor Wen praised him. Afterward, he appointed Shizhi as Commandant of Justice.

Soon after, the emperor traveled out through the Zhongwei Bridge, and a man came out from under the bridge, startling the horses of the imperial carriage. The emperor then sent riders to capture him and handed him over to the Commandant of Justice. Shizhi interrogated him. The man said, "I am a county man. Hearing the imperial procession, I hid under the bridge. After a long time, thinking the procession had passed, I came out, and seeing the imperial carriage and riders, I ran away." The Commandant of Justice submitted the verdict: one who violates the imperial procession should be fined. Emperor Wen was angry and said, "This man personally startled my horses. Fortunately, my horses are gentle; if they had been other horses, would I not have been injured? And you, the Commandant of Justice, only fine him!" Shizhi said, "The law is something the Son of Heaven shares with all under heaven. Now the law is thus, and if we make it heavier, the law will not be trusted by the people. Moreover, at the time, if Your Majesty had ordered him executed immediately, that would have been done. But now that he has been handed over to the Commandant of Justice, the Commandant of Justice is the balance of all under heaven. If it tilts once, then all those who apply the law will make it light or heavy according to their will, and where will the people place their hands and feet? I beg Your Majesty to consider this." After a long time, the emperor said, "The Commandant of Justice's verdict is correct."

Later, someone stole the jade ring placed before the throne in the Gaozu Temple. He was captured, and Emperor Wen was angry, handing him over to the Commandant of Justice for trial. Shizhi, according to the law, submitted a memorial that those who steal objects used in the ancestral temple should be punished by public execution in the market. The emperor was greatly enraged and said, "This man is without morality, stealing the vessel of the former emperor's temple. I handed him over to the Commandant of Justice to have him executed along with his family, but you submit a verdict according to the law, which is not my intention to honor the ancestral temple." Shizhi removed his cap, kowtowed, and apologized, saying, "The law is thus, and it is sufficient. Moreover, for equal crimes, the punishment is differentiated by the degree of offense. Now if we execute the family of one who steals from the ancestral temple, then if, by a chance of one in ten thousand, a foolish commoner takes a handful of earth from Changling, what law would Your Majesty add to that?" After a long time, Emperor Wen discussed it with the Empress Dowager and then allowed the Commandant of Justice's verdict. At that time, the Commandant of the Guards, the Marquis of Tiao, Zhou Yafu, and the Chancellor of Liang, the Marquis of Shandu, Wang Tiankai, saw that Shizhi held his arguments fairly, and they became friends with him. Because of this, Zhang, the Commandant of Justice, was praised throughout the empire.

Later, Emperor Wen died, and Emperor Jing ascended the throne. Shizhi, fearing, claimed illness. He wanted to resign and leave, but feared the great punishment would come; he wanted to appear and apologize, but did not know what would happen. Following Wang Sheng's plan, he finally appeared and apologized, and Emperor Jing did not reproach him.

Wang Sheng was skilled in the teachings of the Yellow Emperor and Laozi, and was a recluse. He was once summoned to the court, where the Three Dukes and Nine Ministers all gathered standing. Wang Sheng, an old man, said, "My sock is loose," and turning to Zhang, the Commandant of Justice, said, "Tie my sock for me!" Shizhi knelt and tied it. Afterward, someone said to Wang Sheng, "Why did you humiliate Zhang, the Commandant of Justice, in court, making him kneel to tie your sock?" Wang Sheng said, "I am old and lowly, and I reckon I can never be of use to Zhang, the Commandant of Justice. Zhang, the Commandant of Justice, is now a famous minister of the empire. I deliberately humiliated him, making him kneel to tie my sock, to honor him." When the lords heard this, they considered Wang Sheng worthy and respected Zhang, the Commandant of Justice.

Zhang, the Commandant of Justice, served Emperor Jing for more than a year, then became the Chancellor of the King of Huainan, still because of his previous offense. After a long time, Shizhi died. His son was Zhang Zhi, with the courtesy name Zhang Gong, who rose to the rank of Grandee but was dismissed. Because he could not accommodate himself to the times, he never served in office again.

Feng Tang's grandfather was a man of Zhao. His father moved to Dai. After the rise of Han, they moved to Anling. Tang was known for filial piety and served as the head of the Langzhong office, serving Emperor Wen. When Emperor Wen passed by in his carriage, he asked Tang, "Old man, how did you become a lang? Where is your home?" Tang answered truthfully. Emperor Wen said, "When I was in Dai, my food supervisor Gao Qu repeatedly told me of the worthiness of the Zhao general Li Qi, who fought at Julu. Now every time I eat, my thoughts are always at Julu. Do you know of him?" Tang replied, "He is not as good as Lian Po and Li Mu as generals." The emperor said, "Why?" Tang said, "My grandfather, when he was in Zhao, was a commander of officers and was close to Li Mu. My father was formerly the Chancellor of Dai and was close to the Zhao general Li Qi, and knew his character." After the emperor heard of the character of Lian Po and Li Mu, he was delighted and slapped his thigh, saying, "Alas! If only I could have Lian Po and Li Mu as my generals, would I worry about the Xiongnu!" Tang said, "Your servant! Even if Your Majesty had Lian Po and Li Mu, you could not use them." The emperor was angry, rose, and went into the inner palace. After a long time, he summoned Tang and reproached him, saying, "Why did you humiliate me in public? Was there no private place?" Tang apologized, saying, "I am a rustic and do not know taboos."

At that time, the Xiongnu had newly made a great incursion into Zhaona, killing the Commandant of Beidi, Ang. The emperor, concerned about the Hu raids, finally asked Tang again, "How do you know I cannot use Lian Po and Li Mu?" Tang replied, "I have heard that in ancient times, when kings sent out generals, they knelt and pushed the wheel of the chariot, saying, 'Within the threshold, I control; beyond the threshold, the general controls.' Military merits, ranks, and rewards were all decided outside, and only reported upon return. This is not empty words. My grandfather said that when Li Mu was a general of Zhao stationed at the border, the taxes from the military markets were all used by himself to feast the soldiers, and rewards were decided outside, not interfered with from within. He was entrusted with responsibility and held accountable for success, so Li Mu was able to exhaust his wisdom and ability. He selected 1,300 chariots, 13,000 mounted archers, and 100,000 men worth a hundred gold pieces. With these, he drove back the Chanyu in the north, broke the Donghu, destroyed the Danlin, suppressed the strong Qin in the west, and held off Han and Wei in the south. At that time, Zhao nearly became hegemon. Later, when King Qian of Zhao came to the throne, whose mother was a singer, he listened to the slander of Guo Kai and finally executed Li Mu, appointing Yan Ju in his place. Because of this, the army was broken and the soldiers fled, and Zhao was captured and destroyed by Qin. Now I have privately heard that Wei Shang, the Governor of Yunzhong, uses all the taxes from the military markets to feast the soldiers, and personally contributes his own money, slaughtering an ox every five days to feast guests, military officers, and retainers. Because of this, the Xiongnu kept far away and did not approach the border of Yunzhong. Once the enemy did enter, Shang led chariots and cavalry to attack them, killing many of their men. The soldiers are all sons of common families, rising from the fields to join the army. How would they know about the registers and rosters? They fight all day, beheading enemies and capturing prisoners, and report merits to the headquarters. If one word does not match, the civil officials punish them according to the law. Their rewards are not given, but the officials' laws are always applied. I am foolish and think that Your Majesty's laws are too clear, rewards too light, and punishments too heavy. Moreover, Wei Shang, the Governor of Yunzhong, was charged because his reported merits for beheaded and captured enemies differed by six heads. Your Majesty handed him over to the officials, stripped his rank, and punished him with hard labor. From this, I say, even if Your Majesty had Lian Po and Li Mu, you could not use them. I am truly foolish and have touched upon taboos. Death penalty, death penalty!" Emperor Wen was pleased. That day he ordered Feng Tang to take the tally and pardon Wei Shang, restoring him as Governor of Yunzhong, and appointed Tang as Commandant of Chariots and Cavalry, in charge of the chariot soldiers of the central command and the commanderies and kingdoms.

In the seventh year, Emperor Jing ascended the throne and appointed Tang as Chancellor of Chu, but he was dismissed. When Emperor Wu ascended, he sought worthy and good men, and Feng Tang was recommended. Tang was then over ninety years old and could no longer serve as an official, so Tang's son Feng Sui was made a lang. Sui, with the courtesy name Wangsun, was also an extraordinary man and was close to me.

The Grand Historian says: Zhang Ji's words about elders, upholding the law without flattering; Feng Gong's discussion of generals—how meaningful! How meaningful! The saying goes, "If you do not know the man, look at his friends." What these two gentlemen praised and recited can be placed in the court. The Book says, "Impartial and without partisanship, the kingly way is broad; without partisanship and impartial, the kingly way is even." Zhang Ji and Feng Gong came close to this. [end of text]


